## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical batch ingestion, per-record contracts, privacy masking, and idempotent upserts in a common lakehouse pattern.

## Key Concepts

- Data contracts, masking, bad_data routing
- Iceberg schema evolution, upserts, unique keys
- Test plans for duplicates, new fields, and schema drift

## Code Example

```javascript
function validate(record) {
  const required = ["user_id","event_ts","event_type"];
  for (const k of required) if (!(k in record)) return false;
  // simple mask
  if (record.payload) record.payload.pii = null;
  return true;
}
```

```javascript
// MERGE-like upsert (pseudocode)
MERGE INTO iceberg.fact AS t
USING staging AS s
ON t.user_id = s.user_id AND t.event_ts = s.event_ts
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET ...
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT ...
```
